v1.3.17 (2026-08-16)
--------------------
Fix: passwords (and realnames/notes/group names) containing a space were
silently truncated when a hub is reached via the vpncmd fallback (VPN
Azure relay servers, or any server where direct JSON-RPC is unreachable).

Root cause:
- The vpncmd fallback builds each command as one text line fed to vpncmd
  via /IN:file. vpncmd tokenizes each line on whitespace, same as a shell
  command line — an unquoted `/PASSWORD:{value}` with a space in `value`
  silently truncates at the first space instead of erroring.
- Confirmed by direct reproduction: setting password "has a space"
  unquoted via vpncmd produced the exact same HashedKey as setting just
  "has" — the rest was dropped, with no error from vpncmd. Quoting the
  value CSV-style (wrap in double quotes, double any embedded quote)
  produced the correct full-password hash, matching a known-good value
  set via direct RPC.
- Direct JSON-RPC (the primary path, used whenever a server is reachable)
  was verified unaffected — it sends the password as a single JSON string
  field, so hashes matched exactly across CreateUser/SetUser/native
  vpncmd for every test password tried, spaces and symbols included.

Fix — added _vc_q(), quotes a value for a vpncmd script line, and applied
it everywhere free text is interpolated into a vpncmd fallback command:
CreateUser/SetUser (password, realname, note, group, and the username
itself), DeleteUser/GetUser (username), CreateGroup/DeleteGroup (name,
realname, note), SetHub (admin password), SetIPsecServices (PSK,
default hub). The username itself was included because the same
truncation applies there too — see the v1.3.16 fix for usernames with
spaces, which covered the direct-RPC side of that same class of bug.
